  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1252 PM CDT THU OCT 15 2026\n\nNear record high temperatures remain in the forecast for the next\ntwo days, as the region remains situated under a stout ridge at\nthe surface, and the upper-levels. Thankfully, this ridging\npattern will begin to break down into Friday evening, as a very\ndeep trough and cold front begin to move SE through the Midwest.\nWinds ahead of, and behind this cold front will be rather strong,\nas the pressure gradient becomes quite tight. Sustained winds\nthrough the weekend will range between 10-15 MPH, with gusts over\n25+ MPH at times.\n\nIt's these stiff winds that will quickly usher in much cooler\nweather, with overnight lows on Friday dropping into the mid-40s\nand low-50s. Highs through the weekend will generally range in\nthe 60's, with lows generally ranging 40's. By Monday,\ntemperatures will begin to slowly rebound, as weak upper-level\nridging begins to reestablish itself off to our west. Thankfully,\nthis rise in temperature is expected to be gradual, with only\nupper-70s expected by the middle of next week. Limited rainfall\nis expected the next seven days as well, with only low shower\nchances on Friday ahead of the passing cold front due to limited\nmoisture.\n\n/44/",
